From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Henrik Klee <henrik@goat-code.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 21:47:23 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] power: supply: apple_tristar_charger: Detect BC1.2 charging
 ports
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Computers, hubs and generic chargers carry no Apple divider, so they were
held at 500 mA even when they are BC1.2 charging ports. iOS runs BC1.2
primary and secondary detection for such a source on the oriented USB
pair: with the measured line routed to the ADC, the Tristar excites the
pair for 40 ms through register 0x03 (9 while D- is measured, 10 while D+
is, plus 2 on the high bank). A primary D- of 326 to 899 mV means a
charging port, and the secondary D+ tells a CDP (below 326 mV) from a DCP.
Both allow 1.5 A.

Run it after BrickID finds no Apple charger, and on a USB-C cable when CC
advertises only default USB power. Report the result as the CDP or DCP USB
type. The excitation register is the one the mux restore already checks
for zero, so a failed restore still holds the fallback.

A host enumerates the device well before the one-second settle time, and
identification does not sample during a USB session. Look at the source
once while settling, from 300 ms after attach and before the session
begins, and keep a host's own advertisement (CDP, or USB-C current) from
that look for its session. Everything else is measured again after
settling, and a PMU source event discards the early result.

J71t ledger 6.4 (iOS 5de33e4/5de35dc, Berkay's apple_tristar_classify()).

Reported-by: Berkay Kösebay <brkosebay@users.noreply.github.com>
Signed-off-by: Henrik Klee <henrik@goat-code.com>
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
